    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.simrank_similarity - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.simrank_similarity.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.simrank_similarity()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute SimRank similarity between nodes.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.simrank_similarity()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute SimRank similarity between nodes.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.simrank_similarity()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Compute SimRank similarity between nodes.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute SimRank similarity between nodes.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L35983-L36144){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.simrank_similarity(\n    source=None,\n    target=None,\n    importance_factor=0.9,\n    max_iter=1000,\n    tol=0.0001,\n    weighted=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![SimRank similarity](../assets/graphs/simrank-similarity.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Students ann, bob, and cy in a column on the left, courses math, art, and bio on the right. ann and bob, outlined in the accent color, take the same two courses, math and art (accent), while cy only takes bio. ann and bob are never linked directly, yet SimRank with C = 0.9 rates them 0.818 because their neighbors are alike; ann and cy share nothing and score 0.0.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nSimRank (Jeh and Widom, 2002) captures the idea that \"two nodes are similar if their\nneighbors are similar\". Every node is fully similar to itself (score 1), and the\nsimilarity of two different nodes is the average similarity of their neighbors (their\nin-neighbors on a directed graph), discounted by `importance_factor`. It is used for\nrecommendation (\"users who are linked to similar items\"), finding related pages or\npapers in citation and link graphs, and matching roles across a network.\n\nA known quirk of SimRank is that nodes at an odd distance in a bipartite-like structure\ncan score 0 even when they look related; `simrank_star_similarity()` fixes that by\ncounting paths of every length. This method follows NetworkX's\n`simrank_similarity`, with the same defaults and results.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str, None]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional node (ID or label). If given alone, return its similarity to every node.\n\n<code>[target]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str, None]]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional node (ID or label). With `source=`, return a single score.\n\n<code>[importance_factor]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[0.9]{.fl}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   The decay `C` applied at each step, between 0 and 1 (default 0.9).\n\n<code>[max_iter]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[1000]{.dv}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Maximum number of iterations (default 1000).\n\n<code>[tol]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[0.0001]{.fl}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Convergence tolerance (default `1e-4`), applied as in `numpy.allclose`.\n\n<code>[weighted]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Whether to weight neighbors by edge weight. If `None` (default), follows the graph's\n    `weighted` flag.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [float, dict[int, float], or dict[int, dict[int, float]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A single score when `source=` and `target=` are both given, the scores of `source`\n    against every node when only `source=` is given, and otherwise the full table, keyed\n    by node ID.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If a node does not exist, or the iteration does not converge.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nWith the column-normalized adjacency matrix $W$ (columns summing to one over each\nnode's in-neighbors), the scores are iterated as $S \\leftarrow C \\, W^\\top S W$ with the\ndiagonal reset to 1, starting from the identity, until successive iterates agree within\n`tol`. Each iteration costs two dense $n \\times n$ matrix products, $O(n^3)$, so\nthe method is meant for graphs of up to a few thousand nodes.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~simrank_star_similarity`) \n\n:   A variant that also counts paths of uneven length.\n\n[](`~panther_similarity`) \n\n:   Similarity from sharing sampled random paths.\n\n[](`~jaccard_similarity`) \n\n:   Similarity from shared neighbors only.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nTwo nodes are similar when their neighbors are. `A` and `B` know the same two nodes, so\nthey score high. `A` and `C` are joined, but random walkers from the two, moving in step,\ncan never meet, so they score `0`:\n\n::: {#d15ad732 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"C\"), (\"A\", \"D\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"B\", \"D\"), (\"B\", \"E\")]\n)\nlabel = graph.get_label_from_id\n\nscores = graph.simrank_similarity(\"A\")\n\ndict(sorted((label(n), round(s, 3)) for n, s in scores.items()))\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n{'A': 1.0, 'B': 0.781, 'C': 0.0, 'D': 0.0, 'E': 0.0}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Like Mara\n\n`friends()` links 24 members of two clubs in a made-up town, a chess club and a climbing\nclub, who are friends. SimRank counts how soon two random walkers, one from each person,\nmeet. Pablo, who has all of Mara's friends, is the most similar to her, ahead of her\nfriend Nico and of Sven, who shares one friend with her:\n\n::: {#9446a59e .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfriends = ec.friends()\nlabel = friends.get_label_from_id\nmara = friends.simrank_similarity(\"Mara\")\nothers = {\n    label(n): round(s, 2) for n, s in mara.items() if label(n) != \"Mara\"\n}\n\nsorted(others.items(), key=lambda item: -item[1])[:5]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n[('Pablo', 0.53),\n ('Nico', 0.35),\n ('Sven', 0.32),\n ('Oona', 0.29),\n ('Hugo', 0.25)]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#a0ea2efe .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfriends = friends.set_node_attrs(\"like_mara\", others)\n\nfriends.plot(color_by=\"like_mara\", label_by=\"label\", seed=8)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-e5626bc2:#80afe8;--ect-3b99d646:#709fd8;--ect-8eff6c8a:#406ba2;--ect-975840b8:#86b6ef;--ect-66b03f8b:#618fc7;--ect-17eebb88:#83b3ec;--ect-692e2bbf:#507db4;--ect-5ee57d66:#4874ab;--ect-f8094ac6:#79a8e1;--ect-a2be67a8:#d1d5db;--ect-88fdb3f7:#6b9ad2;--ect-6425850a:#0d366b;--ect-d77ae92f:#5d8ac2;--ect-472663e1:#6d9bd3;--ect-6826a5f9:#608ec6;--ect-9d43aea2:#5480b8;--ect-3c980f0e:#3c679e;--ect-ec386ef0:#305a91;--ect-97f76ec5:#254e84;--ect-e28c6970:#194278;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-33b15a35:#597aa2;--ect-6c7eec39:#4e6f97;--ect-7b967146:#2c4a71;--ect-6970a796:#5d7fa7;--ect-f241547a:#43648b;--ect-e6bd7a0e:#5b7da5;--ect-5aa54a62:#38577d;--ect-17801347:#325177;--ect-117ef164:#54759d;--ect-0fa89f73:#929599;--ect-c0015a99:#4a6b93;--ect-bf50105f:#09254a;--ect-9c85670b:#416087;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-e5626bc2:#22569a;--ect-3b99d646:#3867a7;--ect-8eff6c8a:#7ea1d0;--ect-975840b8:#184f95;--ect-66b03f8b:#4d79b3;--ect-17eebb88:#1c5297;--ect-692e2bbf:#668dc1;--ect-5ee57d66:#7297c9;--ect-f8094ac6:#2c5d9f;--ect-a2be67a8:#4b5563;--ect-88fdb3f7:#3f6dab;--ect-6425850a:#cde2fb;--ect-d77ae92f:#547fb7;--ect-472663e1:#3d6caa;--ect-6826a5f9:#4f7ab4;--ect-9d43aea2:#6089be;--ect-3c980f0e:#84a6d3;--ect-ec386ef0:#96b5dd;--ect-97f76ec5:#a8c4e7;--ect-e28c6970:#bad3f1;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-33b15a35:#173c6b;--ect-6c7eec